                "entry_id": "d0ad3b07-d42c-3c97-8848-f1a8b53882cc",
                "word": "Shenzhen",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "262c9d04-1424-3713-bf30-e7c26c8b8eb3",
                        "entry_id": "d0ad3b07-d42c-3c97-8848-f1a8b53882cc",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A major subprovincial city of Guangdong, in southeastern China."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "68dba344-53f9-4147-829e-4c4d7ffde94e",
                                "sense_id": "262c9d04-1424-3713-bf30-e7c26c8b8eb3",
                                "text": "Encyclopædia Britannica\nIn 1979 Shenzhen was a small border city of some 30,000 inhabitants that served as a customs stop into mainland China from Hong Kong."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "87425788-7190-4300-a783-792afcec5a88",
                                "sense_id": "262c9d04-1424-3713-bf30-e7c26c8b8eb3",
                                "text": "Over the next two years, China set up four “special economic zones” as testing grounds for free-enterprise practices like foreign investment and tax incentives. The largest zone was Shenzhen, about fifty miles south of Dongguan, which quickly became a symbol of a freewheeling China always open for business. Shenzhen was a planned showcase city, willed into being by leaders in Beijing and supported by government ministries and the companies under them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "57e48ece-6123-4fa5-ac25-2a9486c07616",
                                "sense_id": "262c9d04-1424-3713-bf30-e7c26c8b8eb3",
                                "text": "The tiny 0.8-square-kilometer Luohu District was where the Shenzhen Special Economic Zone really got off to a good start."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0545406c-e882-4881-8ccd-0fc407c383d4",
                                "sense_id": "262c9d04-1424-3713-bf30-e7c26c8b8eb3",
                                "text": "The blockage of the Suez Canal in March? No, there is another disruption in global shipping. This time, the problem lies in Shenzhen, a sprawling metropolis adjacent to Hong Kong in southeastern China."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
